Steps for Binary Search in Multi-dimensional vectors:

1. Start with the midpoint of the list.
2. If the target value is equal to the middle element of the array, then the search is done, return the matched index or true.
3. If the target value is less than the middle element, continue the search in the lower half of the array.
4. If the target is greater than the middle element, continue the search in the upper half of the array. 
5. Repeat these steps until the search range is zero or until the target value is found.
6. If the range is not zero but within the individual arrays in the vector, implement the same steps in each of them.

This is how we transform the linear search to binary search for multi-dimensional vectors. As the size of the input increases, binary search will outperform linear search as its time complexity is O(log N), whereas linear search is O(N).

