The Fibonacci sequence follows a specific pattern where every number after the first two is the sum of the two preceding ones. To determine the specific number within the sequence, we can leverage this pattern within a simple C++ program.

Below is an example of a C++ program that accomplishes the task:

```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

long long fibonacci(int n) {
  if(n<=0)
  {
    cout<<"Incorrect input";
    return -1;
  }
  else if(n==1)
  {
    return 0;
  }
  else if(n==2)
  {
    return 1;
  }
  else
  {
    long long a = 0, b = 1, fib;
    for(int i=3; i<=n; i++)
    {
      fib = a + b;
      a = b;
      b = fib;
    }
    return fib;
  }
}

int main() {
  int index;
  cout << "Enter the index of Fibonacci sequence: ";
  cin >> index;
  cout << "The Fibonacci Number at position " << index << " is: " << fibonacci(index);
  return 0;
}
```

In the above program, the user inputs an index, and the `fibonacci(int n)` function is used to calculate the Fibonacci number at that index. For the first two positions (1 and 2), the result is directly returned (0 and 1 respectively). For indices greater than 2, the program performs a loop where it keeps adding the last two Fibonacci numbers to calculate the next one.

Please note that the Fibonacci sequence in this program starts from 0, namely Fibonacci(1) = 0, Fibonacci(2) = 1, Fibonacci(3) = 1 and so on.

