import java.math.BigInteger;

public class FactorialPrimes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] primes = {2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17};
        for (int prime : primes) {
            System.out.println("Factorial of " + prime + " is " + factorial(prime));
        }
    }

    private static BigInteger factorial(int num) {
        BigInteger result = BigInteger.ONE;
        for (int i = 2; i <= num; i++)
            result = result.multiply(BigInteger.valueOf(i));
        return result;
    }
}
```

In this program, we are using `BigInteger` to handle extremely large numbers which exceed the limit of `long` data type in Java. This ensures we avoid overflow issues while calculating factorial.

We calculate factorial by initializing a `BigInteger` with the value of 1 and then, in a loop from 2 to the given number, we multiply this `BigInteger` with the loop index.

The time complexity of this solution is O(n), and the space complexity is O(1), n corresponds to the number for which we are calculating factorial. The solution only uses a constant amount of space to store the results, hence it is space efficient.

