This problem can be solved using a technique known as Difference Array or Sweep line. We start by initializing an array of zeros of size n, where n is the total number of flights. Then we iterate through each booking and add the number of seats at the beginning of the range and subtract it at the end of the range. We do the same for cancellations, but subtracting instead of adding. Finally, we go through the array and for each flight, we add the number of seats from the previous flight.

Here is a python solution:

```python
def corpFlightBookings(self, bookings: List[List[int]], cancellations: List[List[int]], n: int) -> List[int]:
        flights = [0] * (n + 1)
        for first, last, seats in bookings:
            flights[first-1] += seats
            if last < n:
                flights[last] -= seats
        for first, last, seats in cancellations:
            flights[first-1] -= seats
            if last < n:
                flights[last] += seats
        for i in range(1, n):
            flights[i] += flights[i-1]
        return flights[:-1]
```
The runtime complexity of this code is O(n) because it only requires two iterations over n: one for processing bookings and cancellations and the other for computing the cumulative sum. The space complexity is also O(n), as it requires an auxiliary array of size n to store the result.

This is one of the problems that demonstrates how prefix sum/difference array can be used to efficiently handle range update queries on an array.

